.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
.TH PAM_FPAL 8 "November 2026" "fpal @VERSION@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pam_fpal \- PAM module for fingerprint authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth sufficient pam_fpal.so
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam_fpal
verifies the user with the fingerprint sensor, for every program that uses PAM
(sudo, su, login, GDM). It accepts a finger that is bound to the user with
.BR "fpal bind" .
Use it as
.BR sufficient :
if the fingerprint does not work the password is asked as usual, so a missing
or dirty sensor never locks anyone out.
.PP
Remote sessions (those with PAM_RHOST) and users without a bound fingerprint are
skipped without touching the sensor. The module only reads: it cannot store or
delete fingerprints.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI timeout= ms
How long to wait for a finger on each attempt (default 10000).
.TP
.BI retries= n
How many times to retry after a wrong finger (default 3, at most 10).
.TP
.B debug
Write verbose messages to the system log.
.TP
.B quiet
Do not show the prompt and result messages. They are shown even when the
calling program passes PAM_SILENT, because sudo does, and without a prompt the
user cannot tell that a finger is expected.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.TP
.B PAM_SUCCESS
The finger matched a fingerprint bound to the user.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
A wrong finger on every attempt.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL
No binding, no sensor, sensor busy, remote session, or timeout.
.SH EXAMPLES
On Fedora the authselect profile is switched with
.nf
sudo fpal setup
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fpal (8),
.BR fpald (8),
.BR pam (8)
